      "claim": "Pethidine has an absolute oral bioavailability of 0.52 in healthy subjects, above the 20% threshold and supportive of high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C16"],
      "claim": "Amlodipine shows 64–90% oral bioavailability in humans, supporting high F for structurally related ester-containing drugs.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C14"],
      "claim": "Felodipine has low systemic availability averaging about 15%, but it is a dihydropyridine with distant structural similarity and different first-pass metabolism, so it provides only weak contradictory evidence.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a thienopyridine methyl ester closely related to clopidogrel/prasugrel. The closest analogue prasugrel has ~79% human oral bioavailability, and several moderately similar drugs have F well above 20%. The only low-F analogue, felodipine, is structurally distant and its low bioavailability is class-specific. Overall evidence supports F >= 20%.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct human absolute bioavailability measurement for the exact query molecule is present in the evidence set.",
    "The closest analogue prasugrel is similar but not identical; differences in esterase-mediated first-pass hydrolysis could affect the parent bioavailability."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

